From scott@ntgraphics.com Wed Jul  3 13:11:53 1996 CDT
Message-ID: <current-0005.alphant@archive.invalid>
X-Archive-Original-Message-ID: <199607031514.RAA18483@gold.ato.abb.se>
Date: Wed, 3 Jul 1996 10:40:00 -0700
From: scott pincus <scott@ntgraphics.com>
Subject: RE: Ram prices

The bottom line on memory is that you get what you pay for.   In this 
case peace of mind and no loss of work.

In brief what price often brings:
the lesser expeneisive simms often have  used SOJ's (them little black 
thingies, all about the same size).   These babies might work on your 
systems, but if you are having any problems suspect the memory.   High 
frequency of failure

Next up the price scale is the mixed SOJ's.   Ok this can work.   Be 
very cautious.  Often we can not even mix simms from different 
manafactures in the same computer, let along mix SOJ's on one simm.  

Next is no parity.   This works of not.   It used to save you up to $50 
per simm.  Your computer mother board should generate parity in order 
to use these.

Next is logical parity.   This often will work but.  BUT if you have 
speratic crashes suspect memory first.  Saves about $30 over real 
parity simms per module.

Next is real parity.    Issues here are tin leads (I recommend staying 
away from these always) vs. Gold leads.   Next is the quality of the 
manafacture, Samsong, TI, seimens, Micron are the clear best.  The 
worst,  goldstar, any mixed SOJ's.  THESE ARE THE BEST.  

So you know where we come from,  we are a north american distributor 
for the DeskStation Alpha computer line.  We also represent Dynamic 
Pictiures (High End OpenGL cards) Internationally.   We use a lot of 
memory as you may well guess.  We only use the best we can find!

I recommend buying the best memory that you can find to any client on 
any system for any applications.  Why?   Tracking down memory problems 
is not worth the limited money that might be saved.   There are enough 
things to spend our valuable time on, why waste it.

