Subject: RE: The great

Alpha NT-ers,

Geoff Northcott wrote:
>
>I have several systems that use 100Mb Ethernet, I see throughputs of 
>70-80Mb over 100BTx, there is a problem with 155Mb ATM cards that appears 
>to limit NT to 30-40Mb throughput due to some problems caused by 
>conflicts between the NT network stack and the LAN emulation that is run 
>on the cards.

Here is some information hot off the press from "Network World" magazine,
July 1996 edition, page 8 "Opinion" column.  It MAY be available on the Web
by following links from http://www.idg.com.au/online.world - I haven't
checked it this is the case.

The article (as paraphrased by me) says in a nutshell that Fast Ethernet is
based on standard CSMA/CD Ethernet, meaning that performance profiles for
the two will be comparable.  i.e. a shared Fast Ethernet network has a
sustained realistic utilisation limitation of 40% (this sounds about right
based on my study of communication at Uni for my EE Degree), so that
40Mbits/sec sounds about right.

(Geoff said 70-80Mb i.e. megaBYTES, not 70-80Mbits/sec, about 9-10Mb/sec).

Burst traffic may get to 90% of bandwidth, but burst versus sustained are
two different things.  FDDI uses a loop topology token-passing scheme, and
so 90% SUSTAINED is quite possible there, hence the reason why FDDI is still
not looking down the barrel of a Fast Ethernet gun yet IMHO :)  Even for
full-duplex Fast Ethernet, 80Mbit/sec sustained is probably the limit.

They also point out FDDIs inherent fault tolerance, which Fast Ethernet
lacks.  Scaled to the same degree of throughput, the article authors reckon
that both technologies cost the same.  Individually, the cost is quoted as
$10/Mbit/sec for FDDI and $5/Mbit/sec for Fast Ethernet.

Hopefully some worthwhile food for thought.  Any challenges to the
statements made by Network World (once you've READ their article, not my
interpretation) should be directed to Gartner Group people Skip MacAskill
(Senior research analyst) and Melinda Le Baron (research director), care of
e-mail to inquiry@gartner.com.

Regards

