------ =_NextPart_000_01BB6E85.02AD05E0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="us-ascii"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit

	Hello David,

	The restriction is a result of the hardware/firmware design of the 
Jensen, Digital's first production Alpha system.  At boot time, almost 
all SCSI controllers have Intel machine code that is used to initialize 
the controller.  Since the Alpha cannot run Intel code until the 
Operating System is booted, we have a catch-22.  The problem was solved 
in the Jensen by including steps in the firmware to initialize the disk 
controller, but that means that the hardware and firmware must have 
intimate knowledge of exactly what type of board it is.  Later Alpha 
systems actually have Intel emulation in the firmware, which allows a 
much wider range of controllers to be used.

	With regard to your questions:

	ISA floppy drive to run the ECU:  That really is not your problem. 
 Even if you were to configure the EISA bus, the system would not be 
able to locate the boot disk controller, as noted above.

	Substitutes:  There are no substitutes.  Other controllers on the HCL 
may be used, but only for non-boot disks.

	Other EISA machines:  They're not Alpha systems :-).  I do not know 
what MIPS machines do, but they must solve the problem some way as 
well.

	Replacement parts:  My understanding is that Digital has purchased a 
large store of 1742 cards, and has agreements with Adaptec for spares 
should that supply be exhausted.  I believe that it can already be 
difficult to locate the 1742 outside of Digital, as the Adaptec 2742 
(which will *not* work as the boot device controller in a Jensen 
system) has been available for some time.

	I hope that this helps,

	_Scott

