Message-ID: <9607102139.AA0626@smtpgate.cheyenne.com>
Date: 10 Jul 96 16:59:20
From: Dan_Schrader/CHEYENNE_SOFTWARE.CHEYENNE_SOFTWARE@smtpgate.cheyenne.com <Dan_Schrader/CHEYENNE_SOFTWARE.CHEYENNE_SOFTWARE@smtpgate.cheyenne.com>
Subject: Cheyenne Anti-Virus - Detection Rates

Burt Knight recently send and email to this list concerning Cheyenne's 
InocuLAN's virus detection rates.  I appreciate his taking the time look at 
InocuLAN.  However, it would have been helpful if he had used the latest 
versions of the product with the latest signature files.  InocuLAN detects and 
cures the NYB virus and MTE viruses.  I' still checking on Chill Touch (which 
is very rare and is not considered an active virus nor is it found on Joe 
Wells' list of viruses in the wild).  Detection of "pseudo" viruses is 
irrelevant.  Why should we detect code that's not a virus and does no harm?  
Virus simulators are notorious for producing code that not detected by many of 
the leading anti-virus products.

It is very difficult to accurately test virus detection rates.  Even major 
testing laboratories - such as those maintained by major PC magazines don't do 
their own virus testing.  Instead, they rely on organizations such as the 
National Computer Security Association (the NCSA).  The NCSA tested InocuLAN, 
it certified it's ability to detect 100% of the viruses known to be out in the 
real world infecting computers today (i.e. in the wild).  The National Software 
Testing Laboratories found that InocuLAN detected more viruses then Symantec, 
Intel, McAfee, Sophos, or IBM - and that we were dead even with Dr. Solomon, 
F-Prot and Norman Data.

In fact, PC Magazine termed InocuLAN for NetWare, "ideal for large enterprises" 
and Windows NT Magazine gave us Editor's Choice in their most recent review of 
anti-virus software (Oct. '95).

If you have any questions about InocuLAN or any Cheyenne products, please 
contact us at 800 CHEY INC or visit our security center on the web at 
www.cheyenne.com

Daniel Schrader
InocuLAN Product Manager
