owner-alphant wrote:
>Can somebody tell me if Digital's Windows NT is a 64-bit operating   
system?.
>Or it is just the machine that has 64-bit architecture.

NT is NT is NT (at least it is today...)

Uh, that is, today (with 3.1 through 4.0) NT is a 32 bit OS.  The Alpha   
underneath it is 64 bit, in this case.  In some cases, that may not make   
a whole lot of difference, but if you're doing math, or manipulating   
strings, the Alpha can do it 64 (or more) bits at a time.

With Cairo ('98 time frame?), Microsoft will have a 64 bit version of NT   
that will only run on Alpha.  It's primary lot in life will be for VLM   
databases (Very Large Memory) -- where you can load all of your indices   
in memory with more than 4 GBytes of RAM.  Can you say *Crunch*?

Hope that helps,
Aaron
