I have come across this ATI AMCH 64 for a few months last year. And the 
DEC guys can't help. But I have found something. I have been using ATI 
since 1985 from their HERC clones to now. There are a few versions of the 
card out there the most noticed are GX and CT chip set. And of course 
different versions of the drivers, loader and utilities. It seems that 
the AXP side relies on the resolution you set using the dos-on intel 
side. In fact you have to set those things in the intel box with similar 
config as your axp. Like irq, io, rom etc. Try to get to the same hw 
config as the axp in an intel box first set it all up to the way you want 
it. (I mean adhere to the 3 resolution settings in the firmware of the 
AXP. The GX chip set is out the door. Check the big sqare chip on your 
card, if you saw GX, throw it out and waste anymore time on it. (This 
same card will work fine in OSF and is pretty okay (again set it up in an 
Intel box first). Then get hold of an CT card, set it up in an Intel box 
first to our liking. Try a 1024x768, 800x600, and well why not 640x480. 
The firmware will see it then it would be okay. Acutally, I have used an 
CT based card from ASUS and works fine with NT 351. But I noticed that 
the base S3 from NT is seems to be driving the VGA (640x480) Can any one 
confirm this. I had them working okay now by using a CT card. And DON'T 
change anything in NT display*** leave it as hardware default. Then it 
should work unless you get another funny one (like BIOS on the card). The 
ProTurbo - DON'T even try with it with NT - NO WORK. Someone at dEC told 
me only acouple months ago. My problem dated back to the noname 
motherboard and the 150. The same ProTurbo works with OSF (DUNIX). IT is 
the chip set and the INTEL BIOS - aka the executives services and the w32 
subssytem and your emulation in the NT combi. The CT did work fine. Guest 
what an unknown original old used S3805 works with external resolution 
dip switch is running better, no fuss at all.
I hope the above info will help.
Mike Fung
