Subject: Alphas in 32-bit mode under NT?

On 10 Jul 96 at 19:00, Sakovich, Aaron wrote:

> Uh, that is, today (with 3.1 through 4.0) NT is a 32 bit OS.  The Alpha   
> underneath it is 64 bit, in this case.  In some cases, that may not make   
> a whole lot of difference, but if you're doing math, or manipulating   
> strings, the Alpha can do it 64 (or more) bits at a time.

I read in an usenet newsgroup that the Alpha chip has a 64-bit mode and a
32-bit mode and that NT puts it on 32-bit mode. If this is true than I
suppose all the maths will be done in 32 bits. I am very interested in
knowing the truth about this. 

I have ported a chess program to Alpha NT that uses intensively 64-bit
integer operations (bit shifts, etc.). Before porting the program I
thought it would be much faster than it really is. The program on a 21064
at 266 Mhz runs at about half the speed as on a Pentium Pro 200 Mhz
(under linux). I wonder if this is beacuse the processor is in 32 bit
mode. Or maybe it is the run-time of Visual C++ doing all the _int64
operations with two 32 bit integers (somehow this one seems more 
plausible to me).

Can anyone dismiss this rumour about alphas running in 32-bit mode under 
NT with certainty? Anybody from DEC?

Many thanks
Pedro.

