Subject: Re: Alphas in 32-bit mode under NT?
In-Reply-To: <199607111101.GAA11587@foo.garply.com>

Pedro Mendes <prm@aber.ac.uk> wrote:
> I read in an usenet newsgroup that the Alpha chip has a 64-bit mode and a
> 32-bit mode and that NT puts it on 32-bit mode. If this is true than I
> suppose all the maths will be done in 32 bits. I am very interested in
> knowing the truth about this. 
<snip>
There is no 32 bit mode for the Alpha chip that I am aware of. The alpha chip, as are other risc chips, is very sensitive to the arrangement of the resulting object code. Thus, you should insure that the compiler and assembler is generating optimized code. Both the compilers and assembler incorporate a code scheduling algorithm which looks at the code stream and reorders the object code based on instruction latencies. The 21064 chip has the capability to execute 2 instructions during the same cycle (4 in the 21164). 

Also, on the Alpha, all registers are 64 bit. The copmpiler must generate code to insure that 32 bit pointers do sign extend, and that when generating code, care must be taken that stays within longword boundaries rather than quadword. 

Someone else may jump in here, but IMHO, you should be comparing the Pentium Pro with the 21164 chip, not the 21064 chip.  

