From jvert@microsoft.com Thu Jul 11 14:32:35 1996 CDT
Message-ID: <current-0079.alphant@archive.invalid>
X-Archive-Original-Message-ID: <9607111655.AA21078@sauron.zk3.dec.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Jul 1996 10:29:47 -0700
From: John Vert <jvert@microsoft.com>
Subject: RE: Alphas in 32-bit mode under NT?

There is no such thing as "32-bit mode" on an Alpha chip. Full 64-bit
integer arithmetic is always available and is supported with the __int64
datatype in VC++. The 32-bitness of Windows NT is because the operating
system only makes 32 bits worth of a potential 64-bit virtual address
space available. (the lowest 2GB for user applications, the highest 2GB
for the system). 32-bit pointers are sign-extended into the 64-bit
quantities that the Alpha chip uses.

I think you will find the 21164 is a more reasonable comparison
speedwise to the Pentium Pro 200. 
    -John

